4	\subsection{Effective Pipeline Complexity Determines Token Independence}
5	\label{sec:pixel_shuffle}
6	
7	The $\pi_0$--OpenVLA controlled comparison (Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy_overview}) isolates the connector as causal: 63\,pp gap at $k{=}0.1$ with token count and encoder held constant. The mechanism lies in how each connector creates different token independence structures.
8	
9	Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA) apply $4 \times 4$ spatial folding: each of 128 tokens encodes 16 patches via channel concatenation (Figure~\ref{fig:pixel_shuffle}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary_figures}). Removing any token eliminates an entire receptive field; the folding is fixed, so no downstream layer can recover discarded information. Pure linear projections ($\pi_0$) map each patch independently with no cross-token interaction, making tokens genuinely interchangeable. X-VLA's 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart} introduces cross-token mixing that creates partial dependencies (CV $= 0.151$, four times $\pi_0$'s 0.038; entropy $= 0.614$), explaining its threshold-graded pattern; the cliff onset at $k{=}0.85$ ($-30$\,pp) is reproducible across 2 seeds, confirming the threshold is architectural. The narrow cross-seed variance (std $\leq 1.6$\,pp across all retention ratios, 3 seeds $\times$ 500 episodes) confirms that pruning sensitivity is an intrinsic property of the vision-to-LLM pipeline architecture, not an artifact of stochastic evaluation or task initialization. Within the cliff region, a non-monotonic dip at $k{=}0.85$ relative to $k{=}0.80$ (67.8\% vs.\ 88.6\%) reveals that the \emph{identity} of retained tokens matters more than their count: two additional low-importance tokens (L2 rank 41--42 of 50) cause catastrophic interference on 4 of 10 tasks ($\sim$$-50$\,pp each), while the remaining tasks are unaffected. This task-specific sensitivity is consistent with the BART encoder's 12-layer cross-attention creating entangled dependencies that amplify noise from marginal tokens near the critical density. MLP projectors (OpenVLA, OFT) preserve per-patch locality but create partial dependencies through non-linear transformations; Both entropy and CV independently confirm that the connector, not the action head, determines token importance structure: OFT's entropy (0.667) matches OpenVLA's (0.661), and OFT's CV (0.219) matches OpenVLA's (0.215).
10	
11	The non-monotonic relationship between model scale and pruning tolerance provides further evidence that effective pipeline complexity, not parameter count, is the causal factor. Ordered by parameters: SmolVLA (450M) $<$ X-VLA (880M) $<$ $\pi_0$ (3.3B) $<$ OpenVLA (7B). Ordered by pruning tolerance: SmolVLA $<$ OpenVLA $<$ X-VLA $<$ $\pi_0$. The two orderings share only the minimum (SmolVLA); $\pi_0$ at 3.3B is immune while OpenVLA at 7B degrades from $k{=}0.95$, and the CV spectrum (0.038 $\to$ 0.314) tracks connector complexity rather than scale across a 16$\times$ parameter range.
12	
13	\subsection{Token Importance Heterogeneity as Predictor}
14	\label{sec:diagnostic}
15	
16	The coefficient of variation (CV) of token importance L2 norms forms a continuous spectrum that predicts both the class and onset of degradation: $\pi_0$ (0.038) is immune through $k{=}0.1$, X-VLA (0.151) cliffs at $k{=}0.85$, OpenVLA (0.215) and OFT (0.219) degrade from $k{=}0.95$, SmolVLA (0.314) collapses at $k{=}0.95$.
17	
18	Attention entropy explains why. $\pi_0$'s entropy is 0.959 (near-uniform, range 0.931--0.983): low CV + high entropy means tokens are interchangeable, so any subset is equivalent to uniform downsampling. SmolVLA presents an apparent paradox: high entropy (0.952) yet catastrophic pruning. The resolution is that tokens are uniformly \emph{attended} but carry heterogeneous \emph{content} due to pixel-shuffle folding---``differentiated but entangled.'' X-VLA's entropy (0.614, lowest among models) reflects partial differentiation from the BART encoder, predicting the \emph{onset point} of degradation rather than merely a categorical class. OpenVLA shows progressive sharpening from 0.98 to 0.48 across layers (Figure~\ref{fig:entropy_layers}, Appendix~\ref{app:supplementary_figures}), consistent with its graded regime. CV requires only a single forward pass on task-relevant frames, providing a zero-cost, single-forward-pass predictor of pruning tolerance. CV also predicts scoring method utility: OpenVLA's moderate CV (0.215) implies a weak importance gradient, and L2 norm-based selection outperforms random by only 0.8--4.4\,pp at $k \leq 0.7$ but by 8.5\,pp at $k{=}0.9$ where few tokens are removed and the gradient is most exploitable (Section~\ref{sec:openvla_results}).
19	
20	\subsection{Why Re-Encoding Succeeds Where Deletion Fails}
21	\label{sec:learned_compression}
22	
23	Two architecturally distinct modules---cross-attention pooling (22.2M) and a parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M)---both recover $\sim$60--63\% SR on SmolVLA where deletion yields 0\% ($p = 0.15$), identifying learned re-encoding as the operative factor. The flat M-curve ($M{=}16$: 56.9\% through $M{=}128$: 58.0\%) shows VTR redistributes information without destroying it, even at $8\times$ compression. The $M{=}128$ passthrough gains 13pp over baseline (44.8\% $\to$ 58.0\%), confirming re-encoding adds value through feature reorganization independent of compression.
24	
25	The mechanism is specific to pixel-shuffle pathology: re-encoding breaks the rigid $4 \times 4$ spatial entanglement by integrating information across receptive field boundaries. On OpenVLA (MLP projector), VTR yields 0\% SR even in passthrough mode, indicating disruption rather than useful reorganization (Section~\ref{sec:smart_compress_results}). VTR succeeds precisely where deletion fails---architectures whose rigid entanglement demands learned redistribution.
26	
27	\subsection{Offline Metrics Underestimate Closed-Loop Degradation}
28	\label{sec:metric_disconnect}
29	
30	Offline metrics underestimate pruning-induced failure. The $3.4\times$ action error increase (Appendix~\ref{app:offline_error}) is a negative signal, but per-step averages dilute catastrophic events (gripper inversion, L2 $= 1.02$) that individually terminate episodes. X-VLA exemplifies this disconnect: offline loss at $k{=}0.3$ is 1.0014 (near-baseline), yet closed-loop SR is $22.3 \pm 1.1$\% ($-77$pp). Offline evaluation provides directional signals but closed-loop evaluation is essential for validating VLA compression.
31	
32	\subsection{Limitations}
33	\label{sec:limitations}
34	
35	All closed-loop experiments use the LIBERO simulation benchmark, which does not capture real-world deployment conditions such as sensor noise, calibration drift, and physical contact dynamics. Scale confounding is partially addressed: the pruning tolerance ordering (SmolVLA $<$ OpenVLA $<$ X-VLA $<$ $\pi_0$) is non-monotonic with parameter count (450M $<$ 880M $<$ 3.3B $<$ 7B), sharing only the minimum, and the CV spectrum tracks connector complexity continuously across a 16$\times$ parameter range (Section~\ref{sec:pixel_shuffle}). A matched-scale experiment (same LLM backbone, different connectors) remains the ideal control but is not currently feasible: no public VLA model pair shares an identical backbone while differing only in connector design. The $\pi_0$--OpenVLA controlled comparison (same encoder lineage, same token count, different connector) provides the closest available approximation.
36	
37	$\pi_0$ evaluation is currently offline only; closed-loop results may reveal additional degradation not captured by single-step loss, as demonstrated by X-VLA's offline-to-closed-loop gap (Section~\ref{sec:metric_disconnect}). X-VLA results at $k{=}0.1$ are based on a single seed; multi-seed confirmation is pending.
38	
39	VTR succeeds on SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle connector but yields 0\% SR on OpenVLA's MLP projector, indicating that re-encoding is specific to the spatial entanglement pathology rather than a general compression technique. Re-encoding on the well-trained official SmolVLA checkpoint yields 53.4\% SR, below the 59.2\% uncompressed baseline. The gain on our fine-tuned model (44.8\% $\to$ 60.1\%) is larger, consistent with re-encoding providing the most benefit when baseline features are undertrained.
